## Features

- Sounds go to a virtual microphone, your headphones, or both, set per sound.
- Your real microphone is mixed in, so people still hear you while clips play.
- Global hotkeys, including on Wayland (KDE Plasma 6, GNOME 48+, Hyprland, COSMIC) through the desktop's shortcut portal.
- Tabs, search, drag to reorder, and colors.
- Per sound: volume, trim with a waveform editor, loop, and what happens on a second press (overlap, restart, toggle, or stop everything else).
- Loudness normalization (EBU R128) and a limiter on the voice output, so stacked clips don't clip.
- A phone remote: turn it on in Settings and scan the QR code.
- Runs from the system tray.

## Installation

Download the archive for your platform from the [latest release]({{ project.url }}/releases/latest).

### Linux

Needs PipeWire (the default audio server on current Fedora, Ubuntu, Arch and most other distros).

```sh
tar xzf soundboard-*-x86_64-linux.tar.gz
cd soundboard-*-x86_64-linux
./soundboard
```

To add it to your app menu, copy `soundboard` to `~/.local/bin/`, `soundboard.desktop` to
`~/.local/share/applications/` and `soundboard.png` to `~/.local/share/icons/hicolor/256x256/apps/`.

### Windows

Windows has no built-in way for an app to create a microphone, so install a virtual audio cable first:
[VB-Cable](https://vb-audio.com/Cable/) (free) or
[Virtual-Audio-Driver](https://github.com/VirtualDrivers/Virtual-Audio-Driver). Then unzip
`soundboard-*-x86_64-windows.zip` and run `soundboard.exe`. Without a cable, sounds still play
through your speakers.
